//! P3 resident and resident-order acceptance through one real component and one
//! real world.
//!
//! The component is the repository's own example plugin, built to
//! `wasm32-unknown-unknown` and encoded as a component exactly the way a published
//! package is; the world is a real directory the server opens, saves and reads
//! back, generated by the world's own terrain generator. Two packages of that
//! component run: the owner that holds the resident, and an observer that never
//! does. A real client logs in over the wire and runs the fixture's `resident` root
//! one action at a time, and it runs the observer's `resident-fence` root with the
//! owner's own handle.
//!
//! The settlement profile the owner reserves a site from is the server's own
//! deployed profile: the catalog this test writes beside the component is the
//! authored catalog of a settlement package, declared with the two features the
//! profile needs, so core's discovery validates it and installs the real runtime
//! over the live world - the same path a deployed settlement package takes. Its
//! home blueprint anchors its own point of interest on the column the building is
//! grounded on, so the cell a resident materialises in is the terrain's own
//! surface rather than a spot this test guessed at.
//!
//! The sequence proves, through the owners' own typed answers and the world's own
//! files:
//!
//! * a site listing names an authored site and a free home point of interest with
//!   the cells it stands on, and the reservation that cell answers with a spawn
//!   site token the fixture never derived;
//! * the spawn materialises the server's own villager there: the answer is a
//!   resident snapshot with a handle keyed to this plugin, and it is the only
//!   answer that creates one - a refused spawn hands its reservation back;
//! * a point-of-interest binding commits at the revision the spawn answered, a
//!   binding at the revision that preceded it is refused `stale-revision` with the
//!   binding untouched, and the next binding commits at the revision the refusal
//!   did not move;
//! * a claim of the very villager the spawn materialised, by that entity's own
//!   stable uuid and at the revision the committed binding answered, is answered
//!   with the same resident this plugin holds, and a claim of it at the revision
//!   that preceded the binding is refused `stale-revision` with nothing written;
//! * a haul of an item the worker does not hold pauses `missing_input` and moves
//!   nothing; after a real owned-inventory transfer puts one emerald in its carry,
//!   the same haul commits exactly that item into its equipment and reports the
//!   real change; a work revision that already moved is refused with nothing
//!   applied, and a cancellation answers the revision its record now holds;
//! * a follow order commits with the member applied and a formation slot, an order
//!   at the revision that preceded it is refused as a `blocked` batch whose member
//!   detail is `stale-revision`, and the cancellation commits against the member
//!   list;
//! * the observer's four asks for the owner's resident are refused by the owners'
//!   own reasons - `forbidden` for the point-of-interest call, `not_found` for the
//!   work call, the same `blocked` batch with its per-member detail for the order,
//!   and `stale-revision` for a claim naming a revision only the plugin that holds
//!   the record may name - and the owner's resident still commits a binding at the
//!   revision it held, which is what makes those refusals mean nothing was written;
//! * a demobilisation answers `demobilizing` with its own typed reason and keeps
//!   the handle, and the gear the worker was holding is still on its canonical
//!   slot afterwards - read back through the owned-inventory owner, not from a
//!   value the fixture remembered;
//! * the save path's own player file holds one emerald fewer than it started with,
//!   because the withdrawal really moved one into the resident's carry.
//!
//! A second, non-network case pins the capability boundary: the very record the
//! fixture sends converts under a manifest that declares `persistent_residents`
//! and is refused by *name* under one that does not.

